this was a boy let down neglected and  failed by A system that was supposed to  protect him a wabashak just turned two  before he died in December 2020 from a  severe respiratory condition caused by  this  mold and damp engulfed the one bedroom  flat where he lived with his family in  every room the black fungus led to a wob  having severe breathing difficulties but  despite the family's repeated complaints  since 2017 nothing was done to protect  him almost two years on from his death  and today a coroner ruled action to  treat and prevent the mold wasn't taken  outside the court awab's mum and dad had  a message for those responsible we  shouted out as loudly as we could but  despite making all those efforts every  night we will be coming back to the same  problem stop discriminating stop being  racist stop providing unfair treatment  to people coming from a grad stop  housing people in homes you know are  unfit for human habitation but it was  the coroner's comments which will Echo  across the housing sector throughout the  country  how does this happen how in the UK in  2020 does a two-year-old child die due  to exposure to mold Miss Kearsley said  this issue is not simply a Rochdale  problem nor is damp and mold simply a  social housing problem it is a  significant issue in private sector too  there is little doubt that the tragic  death of a wabashak will and should be a  defining moment for the housing sector  issues of mold and damp are still a  major problem on this estate inside some  of the flats we've visited living  conditions are incredibly poor and the  news of the coroner's conclusion is a  worry for many  nasif was too embarrassed to show his  face but he wanted us to see how he's  been living for the last eight years  with the mold affecting his health too  he told me it's really terrible to live  here how on Earth can a normal person  live in a flat like this it's really  difficult and in another five or ten  years if I carry on living here I think  I'll be dead too the Housing Association  told us they've tried to gain access to  the flat and will urgently contact  Nassif again to carry out treatment  works but responding to a wabashak's  family's accusations and the coroner's  findings Rochdale Borough whitehousing  did not apologize for their mistakes  they said as a community-owned  organization we support the diverse  communities of Rochdale we must make  sure this can never happen again awab's  death needs to be a wake-up call for  everyone in housing social care and  health  awab was a boy full of smiles life and  laughter but whilst his death leaves a  huge void his family are hoping it isn't  in vain in saman Rashid Sky News  Rochdale
 

